QMD for BLDC motor 1 (how and why)

Short answer: yes — you can make a typical BLDC noticeably more efficient with a QMD-style controller using only existing, off-the-shelf sensors (3 Halls + basic current/temperature/BMS telemetry). The gains won’t be magic, but they can be substantial and practical — especially for systems where the stock controller is conservative or uses coarse commutation. Below I outline how, why, and exactly what to implement/test to get the biggest real-world impact, plus concrete algorithms, safety rules, and an experimental plan.

1) Why QMD helps a stock BLDC (intuition)
QMD (as you’ve framed it) leverages phase-coherent control: it searches for and enforces commutation/drive sequences that produce constructive field interactions and forbid destructive ones. For a BLDC that means:
    • Minimizing destructive phase overlap and timing errors (deadtime, phase lag) that waste current as heat.
    • Applying phase advance/retard (fine timing offsets) to track the rotor-field optimum across speed/load/temperature.
    • Using multi-sensor context (temperature, battery state, measured currents) to adapt operating point for best torque per amp and lower loss.
All of that is achievable with hall timing + current + temp + BMS signals: you do not need optical encoders or exotic sensors to get meaningful improvement.
Expected practical benefits:
    • Higher torque per amp (lower delivered current for same torque).
    • Lower copper losses (I²R) for the same mechanical output.
    • Better regenerative behavior and battery-aware power management.
    • Thermal headroom (less coil heating) → longer sustained power and higher effective range.
Gains depend on baseline controller quality; rough realistic improvements: 5–20% energy per trip in favorable cases; bigger if stock controller is poor.

2) Highest-impact, practical interventions (ranked)
    1. Fine phase-advance/phase-search (constructive phase locking)
        ◦ Add a continuous micro-probe that nudges commutation timing ±Δφ and measures torque/back-EMF/current to find the phase that maximizes torque per amp. Non-blocking ISR approach (your probe FSM) fits perfectly.
        ◦ Big win because most stock controllers use fixed timing or coarse hall interpolation.
    2. Real-time thermal derating + dynamic current setpoint
        ◦ Use motor temperature and battery/bms temps to reduce I_set or alter target torque so losses and coil temp stay within margin, preserving long-term efficiency.
    3. Current waveform shaping (reduce destructive overlap)
        ◦ Where hardware allows, modulate PWM timing to reduce simultaneous phase conduction or to center phase current on the instant of max torque. Reduces copper/core losses.
    4. Adaptive dead-time compensation
        ◦ Measure small back-EMF signature or use current transients to estimate MOSFET switching delays and compensate, reducing dissipation from inappropriate commutation windows.
    5. Battery-aware power scheduling
        ◦ Use BMS telemetry (available packs) to cap instantaneous power, optimize regen profile, and avoid battery inefficiencies (high-IR losses). That yields better delivered range for the pack.
    6. Lightweight sensor fusion / observer for better rotor angle estimate
        ◦ Hall-interpolated angle with a simple observer (phase-locked loop or slope-corrector) gives finer commutation without extra sensors.
    7. Passive thermal design improvements (graphoil + minimal heat pipes + fins)
        ◦ Thermal upgrades enable higher continuous current (not strictly QMD, but multiplies its benefit).
    8. Switch to faster switching MOSFETs (SiC → GaN where feasible)
        ◦ Reduces conduction and switching losses so the controller’s fine timing yields more net gains.

3) Concrete control algorithm (MCU-friendly, uses only existing sensors)
Overview: inner current loop per coil, outer speed/torque loop. QMD block sits between outer loop and current setpoints, producing phase and relative phasor magnitudes to be realized by PWM/H-bridges.
Pseudo-flow (executed every PWM cycle or controller tick — e.g., 1 kHz outer, 10–20 kHz inner):
    1. Read sensors:
        ◦ HALL_A/B/C timestamps → compute coarse rotor angle θ_hall
        ◦ Current sensors (shunt or ADC) → I_phase(s)
        ◦ Motor temperature T_motor
        ◦ BMS: V_bus, I_bus_limit, cell temps/voltages
    2. Estimate refined rotor angle θ_est:
        ◦ Interpolate between hall transitions using measured Δt and an encoderless PLL (or simple linear interpolation with speed estimate).
        ◦ Optionally apply small observer correction using back-EMF zero crossings.
    3. DE5/QMD setpoint generator:
        ◦ Input: θ_est, desired torque/speed, recent thermal/battery limits.
        ◦ Output: target commutation phase offset φ_target (continuous), and target effective coil phasors (I_target per coil or phasor magnitude & phase).
        ◦ Implementation: read DE5 parameter table row (based on operating regime) → compute φ_target = base_phase + adaptive_offset(θ_est, DE5_params).
    4. Current controller:
        ◦ Convert phasor setpoints to per-coil current references (I_ref_a,b,c).
        ◦ Run PI current loops per coil (fast inner loop) → duty commands for PWM.
        ◦ Clamp I_ref by thermal limits and battery current limit.
    5. Adaptive phase probe (non-blocking):
        ◦ Periodically trigger ±Δφ probes (ISR) when steady for N ms.
        ◦ Apply ±Δφ, collect filtered torque proxy (e.g., mechanical power from speed * torque_est or current/tach surrogate), decide adjustment direction and nudge φ_target.
        ◦ Maintain history and reduce probe amplitude as convergence reached.
    6. Jitter prediction compensation:
        ◦ If jitter/noise in phase is detected (via torque variance), reduce probe amplitude and increase averaging; optionally switch to more robust (3HB or 2HB) mode.
    7. Mode switching logic:
        ◦ Criteria: torque headroom, thermal margin, power demand.
        ◦ If torque headroom low but thermal margin good → enable 3HB for finer control.
        ◦ If thermal margin tight → 2HB (simpler phasing) or conservative current cap.
    8. Telemetry & logging:
        ◦ Stream CSV telemetry: θ_est, φ_target, I_phase, V_bus, T_motor, mode, torque_est — for off-board learning.
Key practicalities:
    • Torque measurement: if no torque sensor, use electrical proxy: T_est ≈ k_t * I_q (or sum of per-coil currents times a geometry factor). Combine with speed to compute mechanical power for efficiency estimates.
    • Safety: saturate I_ref by min(I_bus_limit, thermal_limit), and implement watchdog that restores safe default if adaptive loop misbehaves.

4) Hill climb → active probe pattern (practical dosing)
You already use ±Δφ probes. Make them non-blocking and low duty so they don’t disturb the rider. Typical parameters:
    • Probe amplitude Δφ = 0.01 – 0.1 rad (start larger, shrink on convergence)
    • Dwell per side = 10–50 ms (depends on system time constant)
    • Probe period = 0.5 – 2 s (less frequent during transients)
    • Averaging window = 3–10 ms for torque proxy
Decision rule: if torque_pos − torque_neg > threshold → adjust base_phase toward pos; else toward neg. Implement hysteresis + step attenuation.
(You have the ISR FSM already — that is ideal.)

5) Thermal & battery integration (practical rules)
    • Set I_allowed = min(I_user_limit, I_bms_limit, I_thermal_limit).
    • I_thermal_limit computed from simple RC thermal model or table: I_allowed = f(T_motor, coil_R, allowed_deltaT). Use graphoil/heatpipe improvements to change thermal resistance constant in that model.
    • Aggressively reduce current for sustained high temps rather than short trips: favor dynamic derating vs binary cutoffs.
    • If BMS reports cell imbalance or high cell temp, reduce regen aggressiveness and peak torque to protect pack.

6) Implementation checklist (software + hardware) — quick practical steps
Software (first 2–3 weeks of dev/cycling):
    1. Implement refined hall interpolation + PLL observer.
    2. Add current PI loops per coil and safe saturations.
    3. Add DE5 setpoint-reading (CSV table) and mapping to φ_target.
    4. Implement non-blocking probe FSM (ISR) for phase tuning.
    5. Add telemetry logging (CSV) for offline analysis.
Hardware (parallel):
    1. Ensure accurate current sensing (low drift shunt + ADC).
    2. Add motor temperature sensor (thermistor embedded in winding or thermocouple on housing).
    3. Add thermal interface upgrades (graphite spreader + fins) if possible — cheap, big win.
    4. Prefer GaN MOSFETs if budget allows; otherwise choose low-Rds(on) SiC or high-speed Si MOSFETs with low gate charge.
Validation & test:
    1. Bench test with dynamometer or steady load; log electrical/mechanical power and temperatures.
    2. Run controlled phase sweep to build torque vs phase map.
    3. Test probe convergence and stability under transients.
    4. Field test on representative trip (compare energy/kWh and temperature).

7) Metrics & experiments to quantify benefit
Collect these on each run:
    • Energy per trip (Wh)
    • Average torque and torque per amp (Nm/A)
    • Thermal curves (coil temp vs time)
    • Peak and average current
    • Efficiency = mechanical power / electrical power (use speed × torque_est)
    • Range estimate difference for same route/profile
Run A/B tests:
    • Baseline stock controller vs QMD-enabled controller, same battery/cycle, multiple repeats.
    • Two modes: energy-optimized (derated) and performance-optimized (higher peak but monitored thermals).

8) Practical gotchas & safety
    • QMD probe must be constrained: never exceed safe current during probes; always revert to safe base_phase if torque drops catastrophically.
    • Do not rely solely on electrical proxies for torque in safety-critical scenarios — add conservative margins.
    • Watch for EMI and measurement aliasing from switching; filter carefully but avoid latency that hides phase shifts.
    • Ensure BMS and vehicle safety interlocks can override the QMD controller instantly.

