10) Bottom line — practical ROI
Given realistic constraints (3 Halls, shunt, motor temp, BMS bus telemetry), QMD-style control provides meaningful, practical efficiency gains by optimizing commutation timing and adapting to thermal and battery state. The single largest low-cost wins are: continuous phase optimization (probe), thermal-aware current limiting, and better PWM timing (dead-time compensation). Together with passive thermal upgrades and modest hardware improvements (accurate shunt, faster FETs when affordable), you can get tangible range and performance improvements without exotic sensors.